# Change Request Lifecycle Runbook

Orchestrator-owned. An accepted Change Request is accepted work, not delivered
work. This page states the order, the recovery paths, and the republish
semantics that used to live only in an operator's head.

The full typed contract — how a candidate is snapshotted, what
`repository_delta` means, what a `stale` revision is, and every refusal you can
hit — stays in
[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md), section
`Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint`. This page is the order of
operations on top of it.

### Terminal Change Request states

| State | Meaning | Sibling producer gate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `integrated` | Landed through the control repository integration/close route | admitted |
| `foreign_landing` | Proven in another checkout by `tree_carried`, `patch_contained`, or GitLab `mr_attested`; repository, protected ref and authority OID are recorded | admitted |
| `superseded` | Intentionally not landed; a named successor exists and is accepted or done | admitted |
| `aborted` | Unaccepted revision withdrawn with an operator reason | admitted |

---

## The Change Request lifecycle, in order

1. **Accept.** The reviewer runs
   `task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'`.
   The element moves to `integrating`. It is accepted, not delivered. Only the
   reviewer run that was handed the revision may accept it. On a leaf that
   carries a surface table (`surface-table.json` or `surface-table.md`
   precondition), the verdict must hold every table row with an empty free
   hunt and no blocking finding, or `accept_cr` refuses with
   `accept_cr_surface_rows_missing` naming the rows — a table row named by
   a blocking finding is not held whatever its label; a leaf without a
   table keeps the legacy acceptance path (see
   [file formats](file-formats.md#leaf-surface-tables)).
2. **Checkpoint immediately.** For every non-final leaf, run
   `task-board worktree checkpoint <ELEMENT-ID>` as the next action after
   acceptance. The accepted tree lands on the Story branch only, and the leaf
   stays `integrating` until Story integration lands it on trunk. Delay here is
   what makes the next producer's Change Request go `stale`.
3. **Checkpoint refreshes the real index automatically.** Candidate capture
   records (but never mutates) the real-index tree. Under the bound run's Story
   lease and the candidate-captured index CAS, checkpoint replaces only that
   captured state and independently verifies branch/HEAD/index/worktree
   identity. A typed
   `change_request_checkpoint_index_unsynced` refusal is retried after its named
   lock, I/O, or ownership condition is resolved; do not use a broad manual
   reset that can erase unreviewed staged state.
4. **Final leaf: integrate, do not checkpoint.** `checkpoint` refuses the
   Story's last open leaf with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint`, because
   closing it would promote a Story whose branch has never reached trunk. Land
   the Story with
   `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr <ELEMENT-ID> --revision <N>`.
5. **When `integrate` refuses, report the refusal.** `validation_not_configured`
   (`BUG-260807-1o5092`, the as-shipped behavior) and
   `integration_indeterminate` for a board that lives outside the control root
   are blockers, not detours. Report the refusal as the blocker it is, naming
   the typed code and the transaction it left behind. Whether a manual
   substitute exists is decided by the repository's own delivery authority —
   the board's `version_control` configuration and the repository's
   contribution instructions — never by this runbook. Where that authority
   requires the hosting platform's pull-request lifecycle, the substitute is
   an isolated clone, a signed cherry-pick of the exact reviewed head, a
   branch, a pull request with a real platform review record, and the
   platform's non-rewriting landing; a direct push to the default branch is
   forbidden there even when it would fast-forward. Where the authority admits
   a fail-closed plain push of the exact reviewed head, that is the last step
   and it must fail closed if the default branch advanced. Do not infer
   permission from skill text, and never route around a typed refusal by
   moving board files or the branch by hand.
6. **A lost run is recovered by republish, never by re-running the producer.** A
   run that died after producing content but before the board recorded it still
   has its content on disk. Republish it with provenance. Re-running the
   producer discards work that already exists and costs the run again.
7. **A republish of an unchanged accepted tree is an annotation, not a new
   revision.** A candidate whose `repository_delta` is byte-identical to the
   accepted one, and whose acceptance still stands, is not a new candidate.
   Recording it as a revision turns the revision count — the only rework
   measure the board has — into process noise.
8. **Never commit manually on the Story branch.** The managed worktree's tree is
   published by the producer's handoff and landed by `checkpoint` or
   `integrate`. A hand commit on that branch makes the accepted snapshot and the
   branch head disagree, and the disagreement surfaces later as an unexplained
   `stale` or an integration that lands the wrong tree. A `task_delta` handoff
   now fails closed on it: the tip is compared to the workspace record's
   `checkpoint_oid` before the validation suite runs and before anything is
   written, and a disagreement is refused with
   `change_request_candidate_committed_past_checkpoint`. The producer repairs it
   inside its own run with `git reset --soft <checkpoint_oid>` — the work stays
   in the worktree — and completes the handoff again.
9. **An accepted candidate that is already on trunk is closed by
   `close-landed`, not by a second landing.** `set_status(done)` refuses an
   element under a Change Request, and `integrate` lands only a
   `story_final` (and refuses a revision an older binary accepted without a
   producer binding) — both at once, and neither is recoverable by retrying
   the other. `task-board worktree close-landed <ELEMENT-ID> --reason "..."`
   is the exit. Without `--review-resource`, the revision must already be
   `accepted`, spends no run, and relaxes nothing on the general path: the
   protected ref is re-resolved fresh and one of three verification methods
   is recorded BY NAME on the ledger with the landing commit (when one is
   named) and the authority OID:
   - `tree_carried` — the recorded `candidate_tree_oid` is the tree of
     exactly one commit that is an ancestor of the protected ref AND is not
     an ancestor of the record's `base_oid`. The second condition is what
     keeps a pure revert out: a candidate cut at the trunk tip that restores
     the previous state has the tree of `tip^`, an ancestor of the protected
     ref, yet nothing of it landed — such carriers are reported as
     `predates_base` and refused with `landed_commit_predates_base`, and the
     exactly-one rule is applied to the carriers that survive both filters.
     Stated bound: "not an ancestor of the base" is weaker than "descends
     from the base"; the two coincide on a linear protected branch, which is
     what ff plain-push landing produces, and the stronger rule would refuse
     a record whose base left trunk in a history rewrite.
   - `patch_contained` — the accepted revision's patch resource, whose
     SHA-256 must equal the record's `diff_sha256` BEFORE any apply
     (`landed_patch_digest_mismatch` otherwise), applies in reverse cleanly
     (`git apply --check -R`) in a clean temporary worktree of the protected
     tip — never in the control root. It names no landing commit; the
     authority OID stands for it. This is the proof for a leaf whose delta
     was squashed or rebased under a tree the record never saw.
   - `pr_attested` — `--landed-by-pr N` names a pull request; its merge
     commit is read from the hosting platform (`gh`), must be an ancestor of
     the protected ref (`landed_pr_not_on_trunk`), and its title or body must
     name the element id (`landed_pr_does_not_name_element`). Recorded as
     `attested=true`, never inferred; there is deliberately no flag for the
     landing commit itself. **Rewritten-twin admission:** when the platform's
     merge commit is NOT an ancestor because the protected branch's history
     was rewritten (a re-sign changes every OID), the route admits exactly
     one ancestor that carries the merge commit's tree AND its subject line;
     the author name, email and date only break a tie among several. The
     tree is the proof that trunk reached the merged state; the subject keeps
     a same-tree squash of something else out. A same-subject commit with a
     different tree is no twin, and two indistinguishable twins refuse
     (`twin_candidates` in the details) rather than pick. The ledger row then
     records `landing_commit=<twin> twin_of=<platform merge OID>`.
   - Foreign repository proof uses `--landed-in <checkout> [--protected <ref>]`.
     Authority is independently reacquired from that checkout's unique remote
     with `ls-remote --symref HEAD`, exact-ref fetch and equal advertised/fetched
     OIDs. The existing `tree_carried` / `patch_contained` proofs then run in
     that repository and close the CR as terminal `foreign_landing`; an
     indeterminate or ambiguous binding refuses before any write.
   - GitLab attestation adds `--landed-by-mr N --repo <group/project>` (with
     `--landed-in` naming the local checkout). `glab` is a required preflight;
     the MR must be merged, its sha must be an ancestor of the freshly resolved
     foreign protected ref, and title or description must name the exact
     element. The ledger records `method=mr_attested`, URL, repository, ref,
     MR number, merged sha, independent authority OID, and `attested=true`.
   - Intentional non-landing uses `--superseded-by <element-or-CR-id>`. The
     successor must exist and be accepted or done. The current CR becomes
     terminal `superseded`, the successor is recorded, and the element moves
     to `done` with the disposition visible in the ledger.

   A `story_final` keeps the tree proof and closes the Story exactly as
   before, co-closing checkpointed siblings. A `task_delta` — a non-final leaf
   landed by its own pull request — tries `tree_carried` then
   `patch_contained`, or `pr_attested` when the flag is given, and closes
   ONLY that leaf: no sibling is co-closed, a checkpointed sibling stays
   `integrating`, and the Story's later final integration treats the closed
   leaf like a checkpointed one (terminal, not carried). Dependents of the
   closed leaf read `isBlocked=false`. A candidate with no repository delta
   is disposed of separately (`zero_delta`) — its base must be on the
   protected ref and no landing commit is named.

   An element at `integrating` with NO Change Request record (the view's
   `indeterminate: no Change Request record`) is the legacy shape and is
   admitted as `legacy_pr_attested` when `--landed-by-pr N` passes the same
   platform merge, ancestry/rewritten-twin and title-or-body naming proof as
   `pr_attested`. PR refusals retain their typed code and never fall through.
   Without the flag, it is admitted as `legacy_patch_contained` when an
   `<ID>_change-request_rev<N>.patch` outcome resource reverse-applies
   (digest recomputed from the resource and recorded, newest revision first),
   or as `research_outcome_only` when `review=none`, `task_class=research`
   and at least one outcome resource exists; anything else is refused
   `landed_close_missing_evidence` naming what is missing. The evidence goes
   on the element's notes, there being no ledger.

   `--dry-run` reports the method, the landing commit and the intended
   writes and writes nothing; a spawned run may use it. Every indeterminate
   answer refuses with its own code: `landed_tree_not_on_trunk`,
   `landed_tree_ambiguous`, `landed_commit_predates_base`,
   `landed_patch_digest_mismatch`, `landed_pr_not_on_trunk`,
   `landed_pr_does_not_name_element`, `landed_close_missing_evidence`,
   `landed_close_not_applicable`, `landed_close_indeterminate`,
   `zero_delta_base_not_on_trunk`. A spawned run is refused as a guardrail
   unless `--dry-run` is given.

   Close-landed and colocated integrate end in a published phase: after the
   board-state commit they push the board chain; a refused push returns
   `board_publication_pending` with the landing intact — run `task-board
   board publish` to finish the publication — and a code landing whose
   story commit still needs its pull request reports
   `awaiting_hosted_merge` with the delivery steps instead of failing.

   The motivating bsim witnesses are `TASK-260914-39ldj6`,
   `TASK-260914-3hcvgq`, `TASK-260914-3s99o4`, and `TASK-260915-3bux52` for
   foreign landing; `TASK-260907-2by6k3`, `TASK-260907-2kf40n`, and
   `TASK-260907-3l4ghl` for supersession. Queued-release regressions cover the
   2026-09-16 freezes `TASK-260916-3e9byz -> TASK-260916-26kksl /`
   `TASK-260915-kkprre` and `TASK-260916-29za6d -> TASK-260916-2h8ii3`: the
   same queued run is re-evaluated after terminalization or `set_parent`,
   without cancel and respawn.
10. **`integrating` is not one state, and `worktree integrating` says which.**
   An element at `integrating` with no owner run is one of four things:
   accepted code awaiting a landing act (`awaiting_landing`), an empty-delta
   record whose only landing act is a board-state commit
   (`empty_delta_board_only`), code already on trunk under another commit
   object (`landed_on_trunk`), or an initiative a human parked (`deferred`).
   `task-board worktree integrating` is the read-only view that classifies
   every such element with its evidence — `candidate_tree_on_trunk`
   yes/no/indeterminate by the SAME tree proof `close-landed` applies,
   `repository_delta`, the deferral marker and reason, the age since the
   `accepted` ledger row — and counts `landing_backlog` as the first two
   classes only. A record it cannot read or that contradicts itself is
   `indeterminate`, never classified. The marker is set and cleared with
   `defer_cr` / `undefer_cr` (below). Route: `landed_on_trunk` → `close-landed`;
   `empty_delta_board_only` → `checkpoint`/`integrate` or `close-landed`;
   `awaiting_landing` → land it; `deferred` → leave it alone.

## Recovering a stranded accepted task_delta

A final leaf whose accepted revision carries kind `task_delta` instead of
`story_final` — published before the checkpoint-readiness repair, or while
the board lived outside the launcher control root — cannot integrate:
`worktree integrate` demands the Story's integration unit and refuses. The
accepted revision is immutable history; recovery supersedes it, never edits
it.

1. **Release.** `task-board worktree invalidate-acceptance <STORY-ID> --cr
   <ELEMENT-ID> --reason "accepted task_delta no longer matches derived
   story_final"`. This is admitted only when the kind re-derived from the
   board actually differs from the recorded one; the accepted revision file
   stays byte-identical and the leaf returns to rework. A record whose kind
   still matches is refused — there is nothing to recover, look elsewhere.
2. **Republish.** The rework producer run publishes the next revision, which
   now derives `story_final` from the bound checkpoint loader.
3. **Accept.** The reviewer accepts the new revision independently:
   `task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N+1>,
   evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'`.
4. **Integrate.** `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr
   <ELEMENT-ID> --revision <N+1>`, which closes the final leaf and co-closes
   the checkpointed sibling and the Story.

### Integration-window lane movement

The phase-A/phase-B lane CAS refuses `board_delta_moved` for foreign Story-lane
movement and names every offending path. The tracked run that passed the
production integration-owner gate is recorded in the durable transaction. An
authorized successor that resumes an active transaction is durably recorded as
the new current owner, under a monotonic owner sequence, before recovery work.
Append-only activity rows whose `actor.run` equals the current owner, and
append-only growth of that run's exact system spawn-log resource, are the
transaction's own runtime writes rather than foreign movement. The old bytes
must remain an exact prefix. Another run, an operator/actor-less row, a malformed
row, a new file, truncation, replacement, or any other lane write still refuses.
See the
[foundational CAS contract](../.specs/integration-lane-cas.md).

## Recovering an acceptance whose integration revalidation failed

When §6.2 re-parents an accepted Change Request and §6.2.1's suite fails on
the merged tree, integrate refuses with `revalidation_failed` and records the
failure on the accepted revision, bound to its subject: `revalidation_revision`,
`tree_oid`, `suite_sha256`, `exit_status`, `completed_at`,
`materialized_commit_oid`. The element stays `integrating` and the revision
stays `accepted`. Either re-run `worktree integrate` (the suite runs again,
under the attempt cap) or release to rework:

1. **Release.** `task-board worktree invalidate-acceptance <STORY-ID> --cr
   <ELEMENT-ID> --reason "fix the failed landing validation"`. Admitted only
   when the bound failure matches the CURRENT revision, candidate tree and
   configured suite and no integration transaction exists; the revision is
   demoted to `stale` and the leaf returns to `to-dev`. Evidence about a
   previous revision, another tree, another suite, exit 0, a missing
   completion or materialized commit, no evidence, or a spawned run is
   refused and nothing is written.
2. **Rework, republish, accept, integrate** exactly as for a §6.2 stale
   revision: the producer spawn is admitted on the stale leaf, the handoff
   publishes revision N+1, the reviewer accepts it, integrate lands it.

## A revision that will never be integrated

Two shapes end a Change Request revision without a reviewer verdict, and they
are different facts.

### An empty delta does not block the Story

A revision whose `repository_delta` is `empty` — an analyst or research run that
changed no repository file — is created `ready` like any other, because
emptiness is an attribute of the candidate and never a state. What changed with
`BUG-260906-37s3ps` and `BUG-260910-1txum4` changed what that revision does to
everybody else: while it sits `ready`, `reviewing`, or `accepted`, it no longer
refuses a sibling producer with
`change_request_sibling_producer_blocked`.

The gate's argument is contamination — the new producer writes into the same
Story worktree the pending candidate was snapshotted from — and an empty
candidate has no repository work product for those writes to reach. Its
checkpoint writes no commit, and its integration is a no-op. Before the fix a
research-only run deadlocked its whole Story: nothing was routed at a candidate
with nothing to review, and every later producer was refused with a blocker
nobody could clear.

The exemption is scoped, and the scope is deliberate:

| Blocking state | `repository_delta=present` | `repository_delta=empty` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ready`, `reviewing`, `accepted` | refuses siblings | **admits siblings only when `changed_paths` is also empty** |
| `draft`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, `conflicted` | refuses siblings | refuses siblings |

The bottom row expects a REPUBLISH, and the next snapshot of the shared worktree
would pick up the sibling's writes. Emptiness today says nothing about the
revision that replaces it. A revision with no readable `repository_delta` —
legacy, or from a newer build — refuses, as does an `empty` label paired with
non-empty `changed_paths`: neither record proves that the candidate is zero-path.

The revision is still fully auditable. It is not deleted, not hidden, and not
given a special state; it simply stops being a blocker.

For an accepted external deliverable, the orchestrator records the landing and
closes the leaf without inventing a repository commit:

```bash
task-board m 'integrate_external(<ELEMENT-ID>, evidence="external MR or delivery evidence")'
```

This is deliberately narrower than `set_status(done)`: the element must already
be `integrating`, its current revision must be `accepted`, and the revision must
prove both `repository_delta=empty` and zero changed paths. The mutation records
the evidence on the CR and in element notes, marks the CR integrated by
evidence, moves only the named leaf to `done`, and recalculates the Story.
Real-delta, non-accepted, and non-integrating shapes refuse without writes.

### Retiring a revision: `withdraw_cr`

An empty delta is not the only revision that never reaches integration. A
candidate can be superseded, abandoned, or published by a run whose deliverable
turned out to belong somewhere else. Before `BUG-260906-37s3ps` there was no
route for that at all: `accept_cr` needs a reviewer verdict and a merged
checklist, `worktree abort` discards the entire workspace, and republishing only
produces another revision in the same unresolved state.

```bash
task-board m 'withdraw_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, reason="why this revision will never be integrated")'
```

It moves the revision to `aborted`, which is terminal on both predicates that
read the state: it stops refusing sibling producers, and it stops withholding
the Story workspace's base convergence. The revision file and its ledger row
stay readable.

Four things it refuses, and why:

- **A revision that is `accepted`, `checkpointing`, `checkpointed`,
  `integrating` or `integrated`** — `change_request_state_conflict`. Those
  carry a reviewer's attestation, or commits that are already on the Story
  branch or in trunk. Recording one as never integrated would make the board
  disagree with git history.
- **A missing or whitespace-only `reason`** —
  `change_request_withdraw_reason_missing`. The ledger row is the only surviving
  explanation of why the revision exists and was never integrated.
- **A call from inside a tracked spawn run** —
  `change_request_withdraw_unauthorized`. Withdrawal is an ORCHESTRATOR route.
  A producer refused by `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` must not clear
  its own blocker, and a producer handed `changes_requested` must not retire the
  rework signal instead of answering it.
- **A revision number that is not the current one** —
  `change_request_revision_conflict`, the same CAS every other transition takes.

`withdraw_cr` deliberately does NOT move the board element. Where the element
goes next — back to development, `blocked`, or closed as a duplicate — is a
routing decision the orchestrator makes with information the mutation does not
have; make it explicitly, in a separate call.

### Parking an accepted revision: `defer_cr` / `undefer_cr`

`withdraw_cr` retires a revision that will never integrate. A human deferral is
the other case: the revision is accepted and will land SOME day, just not now,
and until `BUG-260912-1c46ze` nothing on the board recorded that — the element
sat at `integrating` looking exactly like landing backlog.

```bash
task-board m 'defer_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, reason="why the initiative is parked")'
task-board m 'undefer_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>)'
```

`defer_cr` sets the marker on the current revision; the lifecycle state does not
move. It admits only an `accepted`, `checkpointed` or `integrating` revision
(`change_request_state_conflict` otherwise — an unaccepted revision has a
reviewer to wait for, not a landing to defer), requires a written reason
(`change_request_defer_reason_missing`), refuses a call from inside a tracked
spawn run (`change_request_defer_unauthorized` — a run must not decide whether
the revision it works on is parked), and takes the usual expected-revision CAS.
`undefer_cr` clears it and refuses a record with no marker
(`change_request_not_deferred`): a ledger row for clearing nothing would be an
audit entry for an event that did not happen.

Each writes exactly one ledger row, `kind: deferred` or `kind: undeferred`, with
`from` and `to` the unchanged state, and the row is mirrored into the element's
activity as a `change_request` event carrying that token. `worktree
integrating` reports a marked revision as `deferred` with its evidence intact
and leaves it out of `landing_backlog`. Neither mutation moves the board element.

## Two same-class findings mean a missing regression in the owning leaf

A verdict carries a SET of findings, not one finding — see the Review Round
Contract in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`. The repeat rule reads over sets:

- A class repeats when it appears in the finding sets of two CONSECUTIVE
  verdicts on one leaf, not when two single findings match. The sets are
  compared by class; the mechanisms and reproductions inside may differ.
  Class recurrence alone triggers the regression requirement, even when no
  single mechanism repeats across the two sets.
- `repeat-of:` is per finding and mechanism-based, not class-based: every
  finding in the set names its own value — the prior revision and finding id
  this MECHANISM repeats, or `none` when the mechanism is new even inside a
  repeated class. The orchestrator routes on the per-finding field, never on
  a verdict-level one.
- A set holding several classes still produces ONE `to-dev` transition for the
  whole set. The next rework revision answers every finding in the set, and
  adds a named regression test with a narrowing mutant the test kills for each
  repeated class.

When a class repeats, stop routing another rework revision that will be
reviewed from memory. The response is a gate — but the gate lives in the
OWNING leaf: the next rework revision of that same leaf adds the named
regression test for the class, with a narrowing mutant that the test kills,
and the reviewer reviews the revision against that test. This is ordinary
developer rework with the finding as context; it is not a new task.

Worked example: the verdict on revision 4 carries the set {F2, F3} and the
verdict on revision 5 carries {F3}. F3 appears in both consecutive sets, so
F3 repeats as a class, and the revision-6 rework adds the F3 regression test
and mutant however the mechanisms compare. The `repeat-of:` value then splits
by mechanism. Case A: the revision-5 F3 finding repeats the same mechanism as
the revision-4 F3 finding, so it names `repeat-of:` pointing at the
revision-4 F3 finding. Case B: the revision-5 F3 finding carries a different
mechanism in the same F3 class, so the mechanism does not repeat and the
finding names `repeat-of: none` — while the class recurrence still triggers
the regression requirement. F2 appears only in the revision-4 set, so it does
not repeat. Both verdicts route once — revision 4's two findings produce a
single `to-dev`, not one per finding.

A separate task is created only when a checked spec gap says the class cannot
be covered inside the leaf — the `solution-architect` rule 3 `Justified gap`
record: the missing piece, the requirement left incomplete, the sections
checked. That task carries its own budget from
[research workflow](research-workflow.md#research-plan-contract-bounded-by-default)
and is never a research leaf. "The sibling has a harness" or "the class
deserves a general checker" is consistency, not a gap, and is refused by row R1
of [negative evidence](negative-evidence.md#refusal-rows-research-and-validation-budgets).

Evidence for the bound: `TASK-260902-yv0g58` needed a gate after three
same-class revisions. `TASK-260909-2ax2l1` then showed the amplifier: routing
each `repeat-of:` to a NEW generalized gate leaf produced three nested harness
leaves, 42 runs and 14.62 worker hours with zero production bytes changed. The
gate was right; its address was wrong.

## Review fan-out: analysts per row, one verdict run

A review round fans out by surface row instead of asking one run to walk the
whole table: `task-board spawn <LEAF> --role reviewer --review-fanout`
launches one analyst run per surface-row group in parallel at the pinned
reviewer pair, each in a fresh context with one row plus the candidate,
each appending confirmed reproductions to `findings-<row>.json` without
recording a verdict. One verdict run — a plain `task-board spawn <LEAF>
--role reviewer` once the analysts are terminal — then attacks the diff
since the previously reviewed revision first, re-runs every reproduction,
drops what does not reproduce with a note, merges by mechanism, ranks by
severity, spends the remaining budget on a free hunt, and records the
single verdict; its prompt carries the merged analyst draft in a
`Verdict Run Brief`. Only the verdict run handed the revision may
`accept_cr` or `reject_cr`. The analyst and verdict-run brief templates are in
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-analyst-fan-out-one-run-per-surface-row)
and
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-verdict-run-merge-re-run-one-verdict).

## Loop detector: S1, S2, S3, S4, S5

Every `changes_requested` verdict recording evaluates five loop-detector
signals over the leaf's revision history — the CR revision records plus the
verdict findings arrays stamped beside them, the stored per-revision
candidate patches, and the acceptance snapshots recorded at publication —
and records the outcome on the element as a finding-class note. There are
two recording routes and both evaluate: `reject_cr`, the reviewer-called
mutation, which stamps the verdict findings; and the spawn runtime's
`recordChangeRequestVerdict`, which moves the revision when a reviewer run
completes at `to-dev` without calling `reject_cr` and stamps nothing — the
runtime observes only the verdict branch, so S1 decides on the revision
count and S4 on the stored patches and snapshots there, while S2, S3 and S5
state their bound. What the orchestrator DOES with a fired signal — the
response routing and the spawn gate — is the loop response below. This
section names all five signals, their exact thresholds, the note format,
and the response each recording routes to.

- **S1, revision overrun.** Fires when the leaf's revision count strictly
  exceeds `max(3, 2 x median revisions of the accepted leaves in the same
  Story)`. The median runs over Task/Bug siblings under the same parent whose
  Change Request is accepted or beyond, and doubles exactly: twice the middle
  value for an odd sample, the sum of the two middle values for an even one,
  so no rounding moves the threshold. With fewer than two accepted leaves the
  median is undecidable and the threshold falls back to 3. A count AT the
  threshold does not fire — the contract says "exceed".
- **S2, same class three verdicts running.** Fires when the same finding class
  appears in three consecutive `changes_requested` verdicts ending at the
  just-recorded one. Class identity is the finding `id` — the class slug —
  continued through the `repeat-of` chain: a finding whose `repeat-of` names
  an id present in the immediately previous verdict carries that class
  forward even when its own id was renamed, transitively, so a twice-renamed
  chain still continues its root class. A repeated class with a new mechanism
  legitimately carries `repeat-of: none` and still matches by id. A
  `repeat-of` value that names no id in the previous verdict (free text, a
  misspelling, a dormant class) continues nothing — the class is the id
  alone. A revision that is not `changes_requested`, and a legacy prose
  verdict with unknown classes, break every streak rather than guessing.
- **S3, fix-induced defect.** Fires when a BLOCKING finding of the
  just-recorded verdict carries a `file:line` (or `file:start-end`) anchor
  in its mechanism text that overlaps an INTRODUCED line-span of the same
  file: an added line of the STORED patch of revision N-1 or N-2 whose
  file+content the predecessor patch (N-2 or N-3 respectively) never added.
  The stored `<LEAF>_change-request_rev<N>.patch` resources are cumulative
  base->candidate diffs, so presence in patch(N-1) alone never attributes —
  a hunk from revision 1 sits inside every later patch — and only the
  subtraction decides. Attribution reads only the stored patches, never the
  worktree, and only the trailing four revisions' patches are loaded.
  Revision 1 never attributes, even at N=2 and N=3 where the lookback holds
  it — structurally, since an introduction set needs the predecessor patch
  and revision 1 has none, which is also the intent: S3 names fix-induced
  defects and revision 1 is the original implementation, not a fix. Added
  means added: an anchor on a context or
  removed line attributes to nothing, and a mechanism with no anchor — the
  design statement the verdict contract allows — is out of S3's reach. When
  both revisions hold the anchor the nearer wins. A bare basename matches
  its trailing path, so two same-basename files in one patch share the
  attribution. Anchor positions are approximate across revisions: the anchor
  names a line of the just-reviewed tree while the spans come from the
  N-1/N-2 trees, so later insertions above the anchor move it out of the
  span.
- **S4, implementation growth.** Fires when the implementation size grew more
  than 20% in the step into a revision past revision 3 — revision 4 or
  later — while the recorded acceptance text is unchanged. The size is
  added+removed body lines over non-test, non-doc files of the stored
  revision patch; each stored patch is the cumulative base->candidate diff,
  so the comparison is the growth of the leaf's total implementation delta
  in one revision. Test means a `*_test.*`, `test_*.*`, `*.test.*` or
  `*.spec.*` basename or anything under `test`, `tests`, `__test__`,
  `__tests__` or `testdata`; doc means a prose extension (`.md`,
  `.markdown`, `.rst`, `.txt`, `.adoc`), anything under `doc` or `docs`, or
  a `README`, `CHANGELOG`, `LICENSE` or `NOTICE` stem. The growth check is
  exact integer math — `5*size(N) > 6*size(N-1)` — and from an empty
  baseline any implementation code fires. Unchanged means the acceptance
  snapshots recorded on revisions N-1 and N are both present and
  byte-equal; a missing snapshot or an unusable patch on either side leaves
  the signal silent.
- **S5, unclassifiable in-spec input.** Fires when the just-recorded verdict
  carries a BLOCKING (`bypass`, `regression`, `robustness`) finding flagged
  `ac_unclassifiable` — the reviewer judges the acceptance criteria cannot
  classify the finding's input as in or out of scope, and records the finding
  in-spec anyway. A `note`-severity item with the flag is a suspicion, not a
  loop-driving finding, and never fires S5.

The note is one line per fired signal, each naming the signal, the class and
the evidence:

```
loop-detector rev5: S1 revisions=5 threshold=4 (2xmedian=4 over 2 accepted sibling leaves) — revision overrun
loop-detector rev5: S2 class=F3 revisions=3,4,5 — same class in three consecutive changes_requested verdicts
loop-detector rev5: S3 class=F3 revision=5 attributed=rev4 file=walk.go anchor=42 hunk=40-47 — finding mechanism lies inside hunks introduced by rev4
loop-detector rev5: S4 revision=5 previous=4 size=130 previous_size=100 — implementation size grew more than 20% in one revision with unchanged acceptance criteria
loop-detector rev5: S5 class=F7 revision=5 severity=bypass — in-spec finding for an input the AC cannot classify (ac_unclassifiable)
```

When the `repeat-of` chain renamed the id, the S2 line also names the
observed ids, parallel to the revisions:

```
loop-detector rev5: S2 class=F3 revisions=3,4,5 ids=F3,F3-framing,F3-framing-eof — same class in three consecutive changes_requested verdicts
```

A structured verdict with no fired signal writes no note. A legacy prose
verdict always writes at least the bound it cannot evaluate:

```
loop-detector rev5: S2/S3/S5 not evaluable — legacy prose verdict carries no findings array
```

(plus the S1 and S4 lines when those fire — revision counts and patch sizes
need no findings array). A runtime-recorded verdict states its own bound
instead of the legacy one:

```
loop-detector rev5: S2/S3/S5 not evaluable — runtime-recorded verdict carries no stamped findings array
```

A revision with no `DiffResourceName` — or with no acceptance snapshot —
writes no bound line of its own: unlike a findings array, which the verdict
was supposed to carry, a patchless or snapshotless revision is an ordinary
older revision, and S3 and S4 stay silent on it. A NAMED stored patch that
cannot be evaluated renders one line per failure even when every signal is
silent, because an unevaluated comparison must read as unevaluated:

```
loop-detector rev4: S3/S4 not evaluable — rev3 patch digest mismatch: TASK-260918-eny7s3_change-request_rev3.patch hashes to 001122334455 but the ledger records aabbccddeeff
```

The kind is `missing` when the resource has no stored bytes, `unreadable`
when the read failed, and `digest mismatch` when the bytes hash to a digest
other than the record's `diff_sha256` — bytes the ledger does not bind are
not the stored patch and are evaluated as nothing.

Evaluation is part of the recording: a ledger input that cannot be read —
the revision history, the board, or a sibling's CR state — refuses the
recording instead of evaluating on partial data, so a recorded verdict was
always evaluated. Every loaded stored patch is verified against the
record's `diff_sha256` before either patch signal evaluates it, on both
routes. A stored patch that cannot be used is not a ledger input: the
verdict recording is the primary operation, so a missing, unreadable, or
digest-mismatched patch leaves S3 and S4 silent for the comparisons that
need it — and records the explicit not-evaluable line — rather than
refusing the verdict. On `reject_cr` the note is staged inside the board
transaction before the verdict is stamped, so a refusal rolls both back
together; on the runtime route there is no shared transaction, so the note
is written after the Transition — a note write that fails there fails the
run loudly with the verdict recorded but un-noted. The `reject_cr` result
also names the fired signals as `loop_signals` and the routed response kind
as `loop_response` (empty when no signal fired); patch issues are bounds,
not signals, and never appear there.

A third ledger path reaches `changes_requested` without a reviewer verdict:
a publication whose validation failed is created already `changes_requested`
(`store.go`'s failing-validation initial state). It carries no findings
array, so it counts toward S1's revision count and breaks S2 streaks like
any findings-less revision.

### Loop response: first match wins, fail-closed

Every recording routes its fired signals to exactly one response, first
match wins in fail-closed order — the loop-stopping refusal wins over the
loop-continuing demands:

| Response | Signals | The next step |
|---|---|---|
| `bound` | S5, then S4 | The bound proposal is recorded and the producer spawn is refused with `loop_bound_pending` until a human answers. |
| `design-change` | S3, then S2 | The next producer brief demands a design change: the invariant, a generated property/class test, and the forbidden patch shape by name. No further point fixes for that class. |
| `fan-out` | S1, first round | The next review round is a full-table fan-out: one analyst run per surface-table row group plus a verdict run. The fan-out shape is owned by `TASK-260918-gshfpr`; the demand invokes it. |
| `split` | S1, past a recorded fan-out | The class reproduced once more after the fan-out round: the leaf is split by surface before further rework. |

The bound wins over design-change and fan-out deliberately, against the
issue text's listing order: S1 is sticky — once the count exceeds the
threshold it fires on every later recording — so listing order would shadow
every late S5/S4 behind S1 and the refusal would never engage in exactly
the long-loop regime it exists to stop. Within a shared response the sharper
mechanism wins: S5 (the AC cannot classify the input) before S4 (growth
with unchanged AC), S3 (fix-introduced, with the attributed hunk) before S2
(three-verdict streak). A recording with no fired signal routes to no
response. A shadowed signal is not lost: its signal line stays in the notes
above the response line.

The response is recorded twice. The finding-class note line carries the
revision, the kind, the winning signal and the evidence the escalation and
the spawn gate re-read:

```
loop-detector rev5: response=design-change signal=S2 classes=F3 — next producer brief demands a design change, not a point fix
loop-detector rev5: response=fan-out signal=S1 revisions=5 threshold=4 — next review round is a full-table fan-out (see TASK-260918-gshfpr)
loop-detector rev6: response=split signal=S1 prior_fan_out=rev5 — class reproduced after the fan-out round; split the leaf by surface
loop-detector rev5: response=bound signal=S5 classes=F7 — bound proposal recorded; producer spawn refused (loop_bound_pending) until answered
```

And the `<LEAF>_loop-response.md` precondition resource carries the demand
the next producer brief renders under Instructions — the invariant, the
required generated test, the forbidden shape; the fan-out or split demand;
or the bound proposal filled from the "Bounded leaves" template in
[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md#bounded-leaves). A note alone is
not delivery: the installed binary renders precondition resources, not
notes, so both recording routes write both, and a silent recording clears a
stale resource instead of leaving the previous revision's demand in the next
brief. Acceptance retires it too: `accept_cr` clears the demand inside the
same transaction, so the integration producer brief never carries the
previous revision's rework text. On `reject_cr` the resource moves inside
the same transaction as the note; on the runtime route a fired response
without a writer wired fails the run loudly rather than recording a note
alone.

The `loop_bound_pending` gate is one shared predicate consulted by every
producer-run construction: `task-board spawn` (at admission, before the
first board mutation a spawn performs, on both pipeline phases), `spawn
directive <RUN> restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` (before the
directive is recorded, with the successor archetype resolved first, so no
directive and no successor exist on refusal), and the autonomous
goal-recovery successor (which parks without a successor instead of
cloning). A refused construction creates no run and leaves the predecessor
exactly as it was. Producer is the Change Request store's own class —
every archetype except reviewer (implementer, tester, analyst, ...): any of
them publishes the next revision on completion — minus the review fan-out
analyst rows, which review exactly as a reviewer does and never publish.
Only reviewer runs and analyst-role runs carrying a surface-table row
binding admit, so the bound stops rework without stopping review; the row
alone never admits, so a row binding on any other role (a same-archetype
role with a status contract, a forged row) is still refused. Each
construction certifies the row by its own rule — the pipeline's row global
(set only by the fan-out loop), the directive's predecessor-row carry
across a same-archetype successor, the recovery manifest's row — and the
shared predicate decides the role half.

The gate reads the element notes and admits only when every recorded bound
carries its own explicit answer. The answer grammar is exact:

```
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text="loop-detector rev5: bound-answer decision=<your decision>")'
```

`bound-answer decision=<non-empty decision>`: the `decision=` key is
required and its value must be non-empty — a missing key (`bound-answer
pending — asked the human`) and an empty value (`bound-answer decision=`)
are not answers. The answer line must name exactly the bound revision it
answers and appear AFTER its bound line: a line minted before the bound
was recorded answers nothing, and answering the latest bound does not clear
an earlier one still open. The gate fails closed on every other shape. The
gate cannot verify the author is human; it verifies the answer is explicit,
positioned, and complete, and the line arrives through the operator's own
notes mutation.

Bounds of the route: on the runtime route only S1 and S4 can fire (no
findings array is stamped there), and without a notes reader wired that
route assumes no prior fan-out round — a valid fan-out, re-escalated by the
next recording that sees the full history. The gate's state lives in a
free-text field the gated side can write: replacing the whole notes text
wipes the bound line and therefore also clears the refusal — an explicit
operator act, auditable in the activity ledger — and forged `response=`
lines are self-harm (a forged bound self-blocks until answered;
escalation lines never admit anything). A restart or reroute successor
reuses the predecessor's prompt file, so after the bound is answered a
restarted rework does not re-render the demand — respawn fresh for the
current brief.

## Progressive validation and exact-evidence reuse

A Change Request revision is validated against the FROZEN candidate the
handoff captured, not against the moving worktree. Validation evidence is
bound to the triple of the candidate tree, the source tree (the candidate
with the board prefix removed), and the validation-inputs identity of suite,
configuration, environment and consumed board resources; a record whose
binding does not recompute from its candidate tree refuses
`validation_not_bound_to_tree` before any write, and an unknown identity
revalidates rather than reuses. While those identities are unchanged, a
later check reuses the exact green result and records `reused_from_revision`.
A full replay of the suite needs one of three reasons written down: a changed
input identity, coverage the reused evidence does not carry, or a diagnostic
reason.
A research handoff with an empty `repository_delta` skips source validation and
still publishes for independent review. The runtime contract, its progress
records and its refusal codes are in
[docs/delivery-validation.md](../docs/delivery-validation.md); the refusal
rows and admissible controls a plan is checked against are in
[negative evidence](negative-evidence.md#exact-evidence-reuse-is-not-full-replay).
Retry, resume, recovery and republish continue the leaf's budgets; they do
not reset them, and they do not re-validate what an unchanged identity already
proved. The reviewer keeps targeted adversarial discretion over every reused
result.

### Identical bytes are not re-proven at landing

The same suite must not run three times on one candidate. A landing already
carries two proofs of the exact tree: the Change Request validation log of the
accepted revision and the reviewer's verdict on that revision. The local CI
mirror (the fallback while hosted CI is unavailable) is a third run of the
same commands, and it proves something new only when the PR head is NOT the
tree those two proofs measured.

Skip the local mirror when all four hold, and say so on the pull request:

1. the validation log of the accepted revision shows the full configured suite
   green (no reused-from-revision row that the landing tree does not carry);
2. the reviewer accepted that exact revision (`accept_cr`, verdict attached);
3. the integration landed with base == the trunk tip the pull request targets —
   no re-parent, no `rebase-land`, no cherry-pick, no foreign advance on the
   PR's paths — so the PR head's tree is the validated candidate tree composed
   on the base it was validated against;
4. the pull-request review comment names the validation-log resource and the
   verdict resource as the landing evidence, in place of the mirror ledger.

Any re-parent, rebase, or cherry-pick — and any foreign advance on a path the
PR touches — keeps the mirror (or the disjoint-path named-test substitute)
mandatory, because the head is then a tree nobody measured. A repository-caused
red anywhere stays blocking whichever proof surfaced it.

| Trunk since the accepted base | Landing proof |
| --- | --- |
| unmoved (base == PR target tip) | validation log + verdict named on the PR; no mirror |
| advanced on paths disjoint from the PR | signed rebase onto the tip + the PR's named tests (`rebase-land`) |
| advanced on a path the PR touches | full local mirror of the exact new head |

## Recovery surfaces

- Stopped or refused integration: `task-board worktree transaction show <STORY-ID>`,
  then the disposition surface it names.
- A leaf whose handoff guard refused for artifact naming: attach the correctly
  named `<ELEMENT-ID>_<slug>.<ext>` outcome resource and re-run the handoff. Do
  not respawn the producer to satisfy a naming rule.
- A leaf pinned to a superseded base after trunk advanced:
  `task-board worktree converge <STORY-ID> --reason "..."`. This is the only
  route, and every other one is closed on purpose — `worktree integrate` accepts
  only a `story_final` revision, `worktree checkpoint` re-verifies drift against
  the pinned base, `worktree invalidate-acceptance` needs the revision to be
  stale already, and the pre-producer convergence deliberately DEFERS while a
  revision is outstanding, so a republish lands at the same superseded base.
  Converge fast-forwards the workspace and re-applies its uncommitted delta by a
  three-way merge; a content conflict refuses with the conflicting paths named
  and moves nothing. A revision whose own changed paths the advance touches is
  demoted to `stale` with `integration_base_moved` and its element released from
  `integrating` to `to-dev` — rework, revalidate, review again. A revision the
  advance is path-disjoint from is re-parented instead: same revision, same
  state, `reparent_count` incremented, and its tree-keyed validation binding
  broken so revalidation is forced, which is exactly what `worktree integrate`
  does for an accepted `story_final`. It is an orchestrator command: run it
  from the control root, not from inside a producer run — it rewrites the Story
  branch the run is standing on, and a tracked run (`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` set) is
  refused with `transaction_disposition_not_operator`.
- The same command is also the route when the workspace has ALREADY been carried
  forward — by a hand convergence, or by an earlier run of this command that
  failed part-way through applying its dispositions. The workspace's checkpoint
  and a revision's recorded base are different values, and only the second one
  decides whether a revision survived the advance: converge classifies every
  pending revision against its own base, so an already-current workspace still
  reports `authority_already_current` for the move while demoting or re-parenting
  the revisions that are still pinned behind it. Re-running it is safe and is the
  documented repair for a partially applied run.

## Landing a Story after its last leaf was checkpointed

If stale siblings were closed or moved away after checkpointing, a Story can
have no open producer left to publish `story_final`. From the tracked integration
run bound to the latest checkpoint leaf's accepted revision, run:

```bash
task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr <last-checkpoint-leaf> --revision <N>
```

Integration also accepts a checkpointed revision when every open leaf is
integrating and has an accepted checkpoint in this Story's branch ancestry,
and the selected accepted tree equals the branch tip tree. Empty-delta leaves
use their recorded base as the checkpoint carrier. Missing or unreadable
checkpoint evidence, an uncheckpointed sibling, or a differing tip tree refuses
before trunk moves. No producer handoff, status escape, or extra leaf is needed.

The full Story diff is derived from the branch's trunk fork point. The ordinary
trunk overlap, validation, signing, transaction, and squash rules still apply.
The leaf revisions and their reviewed patches are preserved; the transaction
records the source checkpoint tree for recovery. A reparented candidate is
validated for this invocation without rewriting the earlier leaf review. When
the transaction completes, the selected leaf's Change Request closes to
`integrated` with its reviewed kind, base and checkpoint tree unchanged; the
landed tree is the transaction's, not the record's.

Checkpoint emits a warning when the remaining open siblings are all integrating:
no producer remains to publish a normal `story_final`. It still refuses a last
open leaf that should be integrated directly; the recovery route handles already
checkpointed work.

### Ready Change Request recovery

Ordinary `set_status(done)` refuses an existing Change Request from **every**
source status, including `done`, with `change_request_state_conflict`. A failed
CR lookup returns `change_request_read_failed`; it never enables the inline
route. This applies to operator and spawned callers, apply and dry-run, including
`review=none` and `commit_ack`. Use review/checkpoint/integration normally.

For an already-landed **ready task_delta**, an operator can instead run:

```bash
task-board worktree close-landed TASK-260219-ghi789 --review-resource TASK-260219-ghi789_review.md --reason "repair already-landed revision"
```

The attached task-scoped outcome must contain exactly one fenced block:

````markdown
```landed-review
{"element_id":"TASK-260219-ghi789","revision":2,"candidate_tree_oid":"<full tree OID>","verdict":"accept","reviewer":"reviewer identity"}
```
````

`ready_tree_review_attested` verifies the exact candidate tree against freshly
resolved bound protected authority. At least one carrier must postdate the CR
base. Multiple eligible carriers are retained, sorted; `tree_proof_commit_oid`
is the first witness, not a claimed unique historical landing commit. No patch,
zero-delta, PR/MR, foreign-checkout or supersession fallback applies.

The method records integrated, authority ref/OID, witness list, review name and
SHA-256, reviewed revision/tree, reason and `attested=true`. This is an explicit
operator attestation of attached review, not normal reviewer-run acceptance.
Board status must be to-review, reviewing, integrating or done. Only the target
leaf closes. Done+ready is repaired; done+integrated retries revalidate the same
review binding without duplicating the ledger. Spawned apply refuses; spawned
`--dry-run` reports intended writes without persisting them. Unsupported CR
kinds/states and alternative proof flags return `landed_close_not_applicable`.
Missing or invalid review binding returns `landed_close_missing_evidence`; read
failure and evidence drift fail closed. Withdrawal is abandonment, not proof
that work landed.
# Resources

Resources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit
CRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under
`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent
element-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.

## Resource Types

| Type | Purpose | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |
| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |

## Commands

```bash
# Create a resource
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Architecture design")'
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Research findings")'

# Replace an existing resource
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="revised findings", description="Research findings v2")'
task-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome

# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it
task-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description "Recovered result"

# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'
task-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm

# View resources on an element
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'
```

## Element-Owned Storage

Resources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each
element gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is
stored below it:

```
.task-board/.resources/
├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/
│   ├── findings/
│   │   └── investigation.md
│   ├── artifacts/
│   │   └── run-1/
│   │       └── output.json
│   ├── reports/
│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md
│   └── revisions/
│       └── 2/
│           └── review.md
└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/
    └── research.md         # outcome
```

The resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose
the hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every
segment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an
absolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are
the only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly
encoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same
owning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write
payloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.

Directories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and
handoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report
cannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.

Local and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and
crash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged
element delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion
currently fails before mutation with the typed
`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD
remains available.

References are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:
```markdown
## Precondition Resources
- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design

## Outcome Resources
- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings
```

## Spawn Integration

Precondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:

1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task
2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically
3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions

## Best Practices

- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning
- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes
- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically
- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload
- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists
- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate
- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage
- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence
- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually
- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it
- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts

---

# Bugs

Bugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.

```bash
# Create bug
task-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description="App crashes when starting recording")'

# Track like any element
task-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Reproduce the issue")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Find root cause")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Write fix")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Add regression test")'
```

